window.PHOTOS=[{"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Adelaide_Type_C_tram_179_in_Currie_St_at_King_William_St_16_Oct_1953.jpg/1280px-Adelaide_Type_C_tram_179_in_Currie_St_at_King_William_St_16_Oct_1953.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1953, "lat": -34.92455, "lng": 138.5985, "p": "Adelaide, South Australia, Australia", "h": "About this photo: An Adelaide Type C tram, number 179, in Currie Street at the intersection with King William Street on 16 October 1953\n\nNearby: RAA Place. RAA Place is an office building located at 91 King William Street, Adelaide. It is a 31-storey office tower, reaching 132 metres at its roof, with each level approximately 4.2 metres in height. From 1988 until 2019, it was the tallest building in Adelaide. Originally built for the State Bank of South Australia, it was renamed after the State Bank collapsed in 1991 as the BankSA Building to reflect the surviving bank's new name. It was purchased by Santos and renamed Santos House in February 1997. From 2007 to 2022, after Westpac acquired BankSA, it was known as Westpac House. In December 2022, RAA purchased naming rights to the tower.\n\nAdelaide: Adelaide is the capital and most populous city of South Australia, as well as the fifth-most populous city in Australia. The name \"Adelaide\" may refer to either Greater Adelaide or the Adelaide central business district; the demonym Adelaidean is used to denote the city and the residents of Adelaide. The traditional owners of the Adelaide region are the Kaurna, with the name Tarndanya referring to the area of the city centre and surrounding Park Lands, in the Kaurna language. Adelaide is situated on the Adelaide Plains north of the Fleurieu Peninsula, between the Gulf St Vincent in the west and the Mount Lofty Ranges in the east.", "a": "Douglas A. Colquhoun", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adelaide_Type_C_tram_179_in_Currie_St_at_King_William_St_16_Oct_1953.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/MTT_Type_F_tram_no._227_at_Peacock_Road_near_South_Terrace%2C_Adelaide%2C_9_June_1956.jpg/1280px-MTT_Type_F_tram_no._227_at_Peacock_Road_near_South_Terrace%2C_Adelaide%2C_9_June_1956.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1956, "lat": -34.9357, "lng": 138.6004, "p": "Adelaide, South Australia, Australia", "h": "About this photo: A Type F drop-centre tram of Adelaide's Municipal Tramways Trust is heading south down Peacock Road, just having crossed over South Terrace. Its destination is the Colonel Light Gardens terminus on Goodwood Road.\n\nNearby: Pulteney Grammar School. Pulteney Grammar School is an independent, Anglican, co-educational day school. Founded in 1847 by members of the Anglican Church, it is the second oldest independent school in South Australia. It is located on South Terrace in Adelaide.\n\nAdelaide: Adelaide is the capital and most populous city of South Australia, as well as the fifth-most populous city in Australia. The name \"Adelaide\" may refer to either Greater Adelaide or the Adelaide central business district; the demonym Adelaidean is used to denote the city and the residents of Adelaide. The traditional owners of the Adelaide region are the Kaurna, with the name Tarndanya referring to the area of the city centre and surrounding Park Lands, in the Kaurna language. Adelaide is situated on the Adelaide Plains north of the Fleurieu Peninsula, between the Gulf St Vincent in the west and the Mount Lofty Ranges in the east.", "a": "John C. Radcliffe", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MTT_Type_F_tram_no._227_at_Peacock_Road_near_South_Terrace,_Adelaide,_9_June_1956.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2b/Former_Osaka_Education_Life_Insurance_Building_Osaka_Japan_1998_1_26_002.jpg/1280px-Former_Osaka_Education_Life_Insurance_Building_Osaka_Japan_1998_1_26_002.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1998, "lat": 34.689694, "lng": 135.503806, "p": "Chūō Ward, Osaka, Japan", "h": "About this photo: 大阪市にある、旧大阪教育生命保険のビル。辰野金吾設計。\n\nNearby: Tekijuku. Tekijuku was a private school of Dutch studies in Osaka, Japan. Ogata Kōan, a doctor and scholar of Rangaku, established it in 1838 during the Tenpō era of the late Edo period. Its official name was Tekitekisaijuku , named after one of Ogata's art names, Tekitekisai . It was located in Osaka's traditional merchant neighborhood of Semba on the main trading route between Nagasaki and Edo. The foreign language curriculum focused primarily on medicine, but also taught astronomy and other western sciences. The school was one of the predecessors of Osaka University and Keio University, through the work of the most notable people Ogata Koan and Fukuzawa Yukichi, respectively.\n\nOsaka: Osaka , ó̞ó̞-] ) is a designated city in the Kansai region of Honshu in Japan. It is the capital of and most populous city in Osaka Prefecture, and the third-most populous city proper in Japan, following the special wards of Tokyo and Yokohama. With an estimated population of 2.8 million as of October 1, 2025 and a population density of about 12,505 people per square kilometer, it is the largest component of the Keihanshin metropolitan area, the second-largest metropolitan area in Japan and the 10th-largest urban area in the world with more than 19 million inhabitants. Osaka was traditionally considered Japan's economic hub.", "a": "Mr.Naka guutarabunko", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Former_Osaka_Education_Life_Insurance_Building_Osaka_Japan_1998_1_26_002.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Citro%C3%ABn_ZX_-_truck_and_auto_dealer_-_Chengdu%2C_China_1994.jpg/1280px-Citro%C3%ABn_ZX_-_truck_and_auto_dealer_-_Chengdu%2C_China_1994.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1994, "lat": 30.5728, "lng": 104.0668, "p": "Guixi, Wuhou District, China", "h": "About this photo: Citroën ZX - truck and auto dealer - Chengdu, China 1994\n\nWuhou, Chengdu: Wuhou District is a central urban district of the City of Chengdu, capital of Sichuan, China. Wuhou District contains the historic sites Wuhou Temple and Jinli Street. The district is bordered by Jinjiang District to the east, Shuangliu County to the south and west, and Qingyang District to the north.", "a": "PLawrence99cx", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Citro%C3%ABn_ZX_-_truck_and_auto_dealer_-_Chengdu,_China_1994.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Winnipeg%2C_corner_of_Portage_Avenue_and_Main_Street%2C_1872_%28HS85-10-23322%29.jpg/1280px-Winnipeg%2C_corner_of_Portage_Avenue_and_Main_Street%2C_1872_%28HS85-10-23322%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1910, "lat": 49.89551, "lng": -97.13851, "p": "St. Boniface, Winnipeg, Manitoba, Canada", "h": "About this photo: Winnipeg, corner of Portage Avenue and Main Street, 1872.\n\nNearby: Winnipeg. Winnipeg is the capital and largest city of the Canadian province of Manitoba. It is centred on the confluence of the Red and Assiniboine rivers. As of 2021, Winnipeg had a city population of 749,607 and a metropolitan population of 834,678, making it Canada's sixth-largest city and eighth-largest metropolitan area. In 2025, the city's estimated population reached 850,260. The city is named after the nearby Lake Winnipeg; the name Winnipeg comes from the Western Cree words for 'muddy water' – winipīhk.", "a": "Robert Chamberlin' Westover Lett", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winnipeg,_corner_of_Portage_Avenue_and_Main_Street,_1872_(HS85-10-23322).jpg"}, {"u": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/Hindustan_Building%2C_Calcutta_in_1945.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled", "y": 1945, "lat": 22.567181, "lng": 88.354476, "p": "Esplanade, Kolkata, West Bengal, India", "h": "About this photo: Original description: \" Hindusthan building, one of the most modern in Calcutta, was built for an insurance company but occupied upon its completion by the U.S. Army. Located in the heart of the city, it is the nerve center of all military business, containing post office, finance office, Base Section offices, air, rail booking offices, a radio station,…\n\nNearby: Tipu Sultan Mosque. The Tipu Sultan Mosque, officially known as the Tipu Sultan Shahi Mosque, also known locally as the Tipu Sultan Masjid, and formerly known as the Dharmatala Mosque, is a mosque located in Kolkata, in the state of West Bengal, India. The mosque is named after Tipu Sultan, a famous ruler of Mysore. Located at 185 Dharmatala Street, the mosque is renowned for its architectural and cultural heritage relics.\n\nKolkata: Kolkata, also known as Calcutta , is the capital and largest city of the Indian state of West Bengal. It lies on the eastern bank of the Hooghly River, 80 km west of the border with Bangladesh. It is the primary financial and commercial centre of eastern India and one of the gateways to northeastern India. Kolkata is the seventh most populous city in India with an estimated city proper population of 4.5 million while its metropolitan region Kolkata Metropolitan Area is the third most populous metropolitan region of India with a metro population of over 15 million.", "a": "Clyde Waddell", "l": "Public domain", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hindustan_Building,_Calcutta_in_1945.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Naftaproduktionsbolaget_Br%C3%B6derna_Nobel%2C_Baku_%285889748933%29.jpg/1280px-Naftaproduktionsbolaget_Br%C3%B6derna_Nobel%2C_Baku_%285889748933%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1903, "lat": 40.392711, "lng": 49.84806, "p": "Nərimanov rayonu, Baku, Azerbaijan", "h": "About this photo: SKC892 Skioptikonbild med motiv från professorn och kemisten A.W. Cronquists besök i Baku- oljedistrikt 1903. Naftaproduktionsbolaget Bröderna Nobels anläggningar i Baku, Azerbaijan. The Nobel Brothers Naftaproductioncorporation in Baku, Azerbaijan. Photo: A.W. Cronquist, 1903.\n\nNearby: Baku Children's Railway. The Baku Children's Railway was a narrow gauge children's railway in the Azerbaijan capital Baku. It runs through Shakhriyar Park on a 1.05 kilometres long line with a gauge of 750 millimetres between two terminus stations. The railway was opened on 10 August 1947 as one of the many pioneer railways in the Soviet Union. It was taken out of service and dismantled in June 2009.\n\nBaku: Baku is the capital and largest city of Azerbaijan, as well as the largest city on the Caspian Sea and in the Caucasus region. Baku is 28 metres below sea level, which makes it the lowest lying national capital in the world and the largest city in the world below sea level. Baku lies on the southern shore of the Absheron Peninsula, along the Bay of Baku. Baku's urban population is estimated at over two million people. Baku is the primate city of Azerbaijan. It is the sole metropolis in the country, and about 25% of all inhabitants of the country live in Baku's metropolitan area. Baku is divided into twelve administrative raions and 48 townships.", "a": "Tekniska museet", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naftaproduktionsbolaget_Br%C3%B6derna_Nobel,_Baku_(5889748933).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Vidyodaya_pirivena_in_1885.jpg/1280px-Vidyodaya_pirivena_in_1885.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 1885, "lat": 6.927433, "lng": 79.868943, "p": "Maligakanda, Colombo, Sri Lanka", "h": "About this photo: Hikkaduwe Sri Sumangala Thera at Vidyodaya pirivena in 1885\n\nNearby: Ananda College. Ananda College is a prestigious buddhist school in Colombo, Sri Lanka. It is the largest national Buddhist school for boys in Sri Lanka, with a student population exceeding 7500 across 13 grades. It was established as the Buddhist English high school by Colonel Henry Steel Olcott in 1886, following the national renaissance which took place in latter half of the colonial Ceylon.\n\nColombo: Colombo is Sri Lanka's executive and judicial capital, as well as the country's most populous city. It is also the financial centre of the island and a tourist destination. The Colombo metropolitan area is estimated to have a population of 5.6 million, and 752,993 within the municipal limits. It is located on the west coast of the island. It is also the administrative capital of the Western Province and the district capital of Colombo District. It was made the capital of the island when Sri Lanka was ceded to the British Empire in 1815, retaining its capital status when Sri Lanka gained independence in 1948.", "a": "Kalegana", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vidyodaya_pirivena_in_1885.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Crowne_Plaza_Darwin.jpg/1280px-Crowne_Plaza_Darwin.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": -12.46545, "lng": 130.842078, "p": "Darwin City, Darwin, Australia", "h": "About this photo: Crowne Plaza in Darwin, Northern Territory.\n\nNearby: Northern Territory Legislative Assembly. The Legislative Assembly of the Northern Territory is the unicameral legislature of Australia's Northern Territory. The Legislative Assembly has 25 members, each elected in single-member electorates for four-year terms. The voting method for the Assembly is the full-preferential voting system, having previously been optional preferential voting. Elections are on the fourth Saturday in August of the fourth year after the previous election, but can be earlier in the event of a no-confidence vote in the government. The most recent election for the Legislative Assembly was the 2024 election held on 24 August. The next election is scheduled for 26 August 2028.\n\nDarwin: Darwin may refer to: Charles Darwin , English naturalist and writer, originator of the theory of biological evolution by natural selection Darwin, Northern Territory, a capital city in Australia, named after the naturalist Darwin may also refer to:", "a": "Bidgee", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowne_Plaza_Darwin.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/View_of_the_harbour_and_town_from_the_bridge_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-View_of_the_harbour_and_town_from_the_bridge_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": -17.529291, "lng": -149.574673, "p": "Vaininiore, Papeete, France", "h": "About this photo: View of the harbour and town from the bridge\n\nNearby: Bombardment of Papeete. The Bombardment of Papeete occurred in French Polynesia when German warships attacked on 22 September 1914, during World War I. The German armoured cruisers SMS Scharnhorst and Gneisenau entered the port of Papeete on the island of Tahiti and sank the French gunboat Zélée and freighter Walküre before bombarding the town's fortifications. French shore batteries and a gunboat resisted the German intrusion but were greatly outgunned. The main German objective was to seize the coal piles stored on the island, but these were destroyed by the French at the start of the action. The German vessels were largely undamaged but the French lost their gunboat.\n\nPapeete: Papeete is the capital city of French Polynesia, an overseas collectivity of the French Republic located in the Pacific Ocean. The commune of Papeʻete is located on the island of Tahiti, in the administrative subdivision of the Windward Islands, of which Papeʻete is the administrative capital. Both the President of French Polynesia and French High Commissioner reside in Papeʻete. It is the primary center of Tahitian and French Polynesian public and private governmental, commercial, industrial, and financial services, the hub of French Polynesian tourism and a commonly used port of call. The Windward Islands are themselves part of the Society Islands.", "a": "Peter Gill / UK", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_harbour_and_town_from_the_bridge_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/Night_scene_on_street_of_beirut_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Night_scene_on_street_of_beirut_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2005, "lat": 33.896049, "lng": 35.484982, "p": "Ras Beirut, Beirut, Lebanon", "h": "About this photo: night scene on street of beirut\n\nNearby: Children's Cancer Center of Lebanon. The Children's Cancer Center of Lebanon is a nonprofit medical institution in Beirut dedicated to the treatment of and support for those with paediatric cancer. The center is affiliated with St. Jude Children's Research Hospital, headquartered in Memphis, Tennessee, United States. On 12 April 2002, the Prime Minister of Lebanon, Rafic Hariri, attended the inauguration ceremony of the center. The center is located in Building 56 at Rue Clémenceau and operates in association with the nearby American University of Beirut Medical Center. The center does not receive government funding, and is instead completely supported by private donations.\n\nBeirut: Beirut is the capital and largest city of Lebanon. As of 2025, Greater Beirut has a population of 2.4 million, just under half of Lebanon's population, which makes it the twelfth-largest city in the Levant region and the sixteenth-largest in the Arab world. The city is situated on a peninsula at the midpoint of Lebanon's Mediterranean coast. Beirut has been inhabited for more than 5,000 years, making it one of the oldest cities in the world. Beirut is Lebanon's seat of government and plays a central role in the Lebanese economy, with many banks and corporations based in the city.", "a": "Jacky Lee", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Night_scene_on_street_of_beirut_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Bibliotheca_Alexandrina_--_Conference_Center_-_4.jpg/1280px-Bibliotheca_Alexandrina_--_Conference_Center_-_4.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2008, "lat": 31.207861111111, "lng": 29.909111111111, "p": "Shatby, Alexandria, Egypt", "h": "About this photo: The Bibliotheca Alexandrina . View of the conference center\n\nNearby: Bibliotheca Alexandrina. The Bibliotheca Alexandrina t eskendeˈɾejjæ]) is a major library and cultural center on the shore of the Mediterranean Sea in Alexandria, Egypt. It is a commemoration of the Library of Alexandria, once one of the largest libraries worldwide, which was lost in antiquity. The complex was officially inaugurated in 2002. At the time of its construction, the reading room was the largest in the world. The library offers shelf space for eight million books, containing books in the Egyptian, classical Arabic, English, and French languages.\n\nAlexandria: Alexandria is a major city in Egypt. Lying at the western edge of the Nile River Delta, it extends about 40 km along the country's northern coast. It is Egypt's principal seaport, the second largest city after Cairo, and the largest city on the Mediterranean coast. Founded in 331 BC by Alexander the Great, Alexandria was one of the largest and most important cities of antiquity and a leading hub for science, culture, and scholarship. Nicknamed the \"Bride of the Mediterranean\" and \"Pearl of the Mediterranean Coast\", the city is a popular tourist destination and a major industrial centre.", "a": "Cary Bass", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bibliotheca_Alexandrina_--_Conference_Center_-_4.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/2009_market_Maputo_Mozambique_3857767593.jpg/1280px-2009_market_Maputo_Mozambique_3857767593.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": -25.966378, "lng": 32.583818, "p": "Central \"A\", Maputo, Mozambique", "h": "About this photo: Municipal market, Maputo, Mozambique\n\nNearby: Eduardo Mondlane University. The Eduardo Mondlane University is the oldest and largest university in Mozambique. The UEM is a secular public university, unaffiliated with any religion, and does not discriminate on the basis of gender, race, ethnicity, or religion. The university is located in Maputo and has about 40,000 students enrolled.\n\nMaputo: Maputo is the capital and largest city of Mozambique. Located near the southern end of the country, it is within 120 kilometres of the borders with Eswatini and South Africa. The city has a population of 1,088,449 distributed over a land area of 347.69 km2 . The Maputo metropolitan area includes the neighbouring city of Matola, and has a total population of 2,717,437. Maputo is a port city, with an economy centered on commerce. It is noted for its vibrant cultural scene and distinctive, eclectic architecture. Maputo was named Lourenço Marques ˈɾẽsu ˈmaɾkɨʃ]) until 1976. Maputo is situated on a large natural bay on the Indian Ocean, near where the rivers Tembe, Mbuluzi, Matola and Infulene converge.", "a": "Rosino", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2009_market_Maputo_Mozambique_3857767593.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/2008_Iditarod_Anchorage_%282311649601%29.jpg/1280px-2008_Iditarod_Anchorage_%282311649601%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2008, "lat": 61.21618, "lng": -149.893426, "p": "Anchorage, Alaska, United States", "h": "Nearby: Alaska Center for the Performing Arts. The Alaska Center for the Performing Arts is a performance venue in downtown Anchorage, Alaska. Opened in 1988, it hosts over 200,000 patrons annually, and consists of three theaters: Evangeline Atwood Concert Hall, with 2,000 seats, is designed for opera, symphonic, chamber and popular music presentations, as well as dance and Broadway musicals. Discovery Theatre, with 700 seats, is suited for theatre, smaller-scale operas, dance, film and musical presentations. Sydney Laurence Theatre , with 340 seats, is suited for theatre, film and chamber music.\n\nAnchorage, Alaska: Anchorage , officially the Municipality of Anchorage, is the most populous city in the U.S. state of Alaska. With a population of 291,247 at the 2020 census, it contains nearly 40 percent of the state's population. The Anchorage metropolitan area, which includes Anchorage and the neighboring Matanuska-Susitna Borough, had a population of 398,328 in 2020, accounting for more than half the state's population. At 1,706 sq mi of land area, the city is the fourth-largest by area in the U.S. Anchorage is in Southcentral Alaska, at the head of the Cook Inlet, on a peninsula formed by the Knik Arm to the north and the Turnagain Arm to the south.", "a": "David Weekly from Cupertino, CA, USA", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2008_Iditarod_Anchorage_(2311649601).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/First_hotel_in_Addis%2C_Hotel_Itegue%2C_1898_-_Flickr_-_Dave_Proffer.jpg/1280px-First_hotel_in_Addis%2C_Hotel_Itegue%2C_1898_-_Flickr_-_Dave_Proffer.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": 9.030666, "lng": 38.754666, "p": "Piassa (piazza), Addis Ababa, Ethiopia", "h": "About this photo: First hotel in Addis, Hotel Itegue, 1898\n\nNearby: Lideta Catholic Cathedral School. Lideta Catholic Cathedral School is a private primary and secondary school in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. Founded in 1954, it is affiliated with the Catholic Church of Ethiopia.\n\nAddis Ababa: Addis Ababa is the capital and largest city of Ethiopia. With an estimated population of 6,287,000 inhabitants as of 2025, it is the tenth largest city in Africa. At an elevation of 2,355 metres , it is the fourth-highest capital city in the world and the highest capital in Africa. Addis Ababa is a highly developed and important cultural, artistic, financial and administrative center of Ethiopia. The founding history of Addis Ababa dates back to the late 19th century by Menelik II, Negus of Shewa, in 1886 after finding Mount Entoto unpleasant two years prior.", "a": "Dave Proffer", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_hotel_in_Addis,_Hotel_Itegue,_1898_-_Flickr_-_Dave_Proffer.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Luang_Prabang_Suburbs_%281491338266%29.jpg/1280px-Luang_Prabang_Suburbs_%281491338266%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2007, "lat": 19.889754, "lng": 102.137489, "p": "Luang Prabang, Laos", "h": "Nearby: Luang Prabang. Luang Prabang , historically known as Xieng Thong, and alternatively spelled Luang Phabang or Louangphabang, is the capital of Luang Prabang Province in north-central Laos. Its name, meaning “Royal Buddha Image,” derives from the Phra Bang, a statue symbolizing Lao sovereignty. Designated a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1995, the city is recognized for blending Lao architecture, European colonial buildings, and over 30 Buddhist temples. The protected area encompasses 33 of its 58 villages, where daily rituals like the morning alms-giving ceremony persist.", "a": "Francisco Anzola", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luang_Prabang_Suburbs_(1491338266).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Athens%2C_Greece_-_panoramio_%2827%29.jpg/1280px-Athens%2C_Greece_-_panoramio_%2827%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": 37.979892, "lng": 23.72699, "p": "Ακαδημία, Athens, Greece", "h": "Nearby: Athinas Street. Athinas Street is a street in downtown Athens in Greece. It is named after Athena, the goddess of wisdom. The street runs from Ermou Street north to Omonoia Square through the Psiri neighborhood. Its total length is nearly 1 km of which 80 m is a walkway since 1999. Until 1999, it used to access with Omonoia Square, now motorists can now access it via Lykourgos Street east to Aiolou and 28 Oktovriou Street and other streets. North of Lykourgos is a pedestrian walkway. It has two lanes of traffic and room for curb parking. Famous buildings includes the Athens City Hall on the west side and office towers northward. The Modern Agora is east of Athinas Street.\n\nAthens: Athens is the capital and largest city of Greece. A significant coastal urban area in the Mediterranean, Athens is also the capital of the Attica region and is the southernmost capital on the European mainland. With its urban area's population numbering over 3.6 million, it is the eighth-largest urban area in the European Union . The Municipality of Athens , which constitutes a small administrative unit of the entire urban area, had a population of 643,452 in 2021, within its official limits, and a land area of 38.96 square kilometres . Athens joined the UNESCO Global Network of Learning Cities in 2016.", "a": "theusmanmali", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens,_Greece_-_panoramio_(27).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Calles_de_Ad%C3%ADs_Abeba_-_2022_-_05.jpg/1280px-Calles_de_Ad%C3%ADs_Abeba_-_2022_-_05.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2022, "lat": 9.043017, "lng": 38.761406, "p": "Arat Kilo, Addis Ababa, Ethiopia", "h": "About this photo: People on the streets of Addis Ababa, Ethiopia.\n\nNearby: Yekatit 12 monument. The Yekatit 12 monument is a memorial in Addis Ababa commemorating the Yekatit 12 pogrom, an Italian war crime in Ethiopia. The pogrom took place from 19 to 21 February 1937 and is known as Yekatit 12 after the start date in the Ethiopian calendar. The memorial is an obelisk in the centre of the roundabout of Sidist Kilo Square , also called \"Yekatit 12 Square\".\n\nAddis Ababa: Addis Ababa is the capital and largest city of Ethiopia. With an estimated population of 6,287,000 inhabitants as of 2025, it is the tenth largest city in Africa. At an elevation of 2,355 metres , it is the fourth-highest capital city in the world and the highest capital in Africa. Addis Ababa is a highly developed and important cultural, artistic, financial and administrative center of Ethiopia. The founding history of Addis Ababa dates back to the late 19th century by Menelik II, Negus of Shewa, in 1886 after finding Mount Entoto unpleasant two years prior.", "a": "ProtoplasmaKid", "l": "CC BY 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calles_de_Ad%C3%ADs_Abeba_-_2022_-_05.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/Mapillary_%28h8fVA5wrgE1CTyODjuQZIz%29_%28skillsy%29_2023-03-24_22H32M23S227.jpg/1280px-Mapillary_%28h8fVA5wrgE1CTyODjuQZIz%29_%28skillsy%29_2023-03-24_22H32M23S227.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2023, "lat": -42.884002258182, "lng": 147.33167005545, "p": "Hobart, Tasmania, Australia", "h": "Nearby: Old Government House, Hobart. Old Government House was the former official residence and home of the Governor of Tasmania from 1807 to 1858, when it was demolished following completion of the modern Government House, Hobart on the Queens Domain. It was located approximately where Hobart Town Hall and Franklin Square stand now. It was built sometime between 1805 and 1810 with dates varying according to sources and at least during 1831 housed the Tasmanian Parliament as well as being the residence of the Governor and meeting place of the Tasmanian Society, a precursor to the Royal Society of Tasmania.\n\nHobart: Hobart is the capital and most populous city of the island state of Tasmania, Australia. Located in Tasmania's south-east on the estuary of the River Derwent, it is the southernmost capital city in Australia. Despite containing nearly half of Tasmania's population, Hobart is the least-populated Australian state capital city, and second-smallest by population and area after Darwin if territories are taken into account. Its skyline is dominated by the 1,271-metre kunanyi / Mount Wellington, and its harbour forms the second-deepest natural port in the world, with much of the city's waterfront consisting of reclaimed land.", "a": "skillsy", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapillary_(h8fVA5wrgE1CTyODjuQZIz)_(skillsy)_2023-03-24_22H32M23S227.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Place_Villeneuve-Bargemon_07.jpg/1280px-Place_Villeneuve-Bargemon_07.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2024, "lat": 43.29625, "lng": 5.370522, "p": "2nd Arrondissement, Marseille, France", "h": "About this photo: Place Villeneuve-Bargemon\n\nNearby: Siege of Marseille (1524). The Siege of Marseille was conducted in August and September 1524 as part of a larger conflict known as the Italian War of 1521–1526 by a Habsburg army, commanded by the Frenchman Charles de Bourbon and the Spaniard Fernando de Avalos. The operation was a failure for the besiegers, who withdrew under the threat of a French relief army commanded by King Francis I in person .\n\nMarseille: Marseille is a city in southern France, the prefecture of the Bouches-du-Rhône department and of the Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur region. Situated in Provence, it marks the eastern end of the Côte Bleue on the Mediterranean coast. Marseille is the second-most populous city proper in France, after Paris, with 886,040 people in 2023 over a municipal territory of 241 km2 . Together with its suburbs and exurbs, the Marseille metropolitan area, which extends over 3,972 km2 , had a population of 1,917,728 as of 2023.", "a": "Abxbay", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_Villeneuve-Bargemon_07.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Church_of_Santa_Catalina%2C_2022.jpg/1280px-Church_of_Santa_Catalina%2C_2022.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2022, "lat": 37.39313, "lng": -5.98818, "p": "Casco Antiguo, Seville, Spain", "h": "About this photo: Church of Santa Catalina, 2022\n\nNearby: Casa de Pilatos. La Casa de Pilatos is an Andalusian palace in Seville, Spain, which serves as the permanent residence of the Dukes of Medinaceli. It is an example of an Italian Renaissance building with Mudéjar elements and decorations. It is considered the prototype of the Andalusian palace. The Casa de Pilatos has around 150 different azulejo designs made in the 1530s by the brothers Diego and Juan Pulido, one of the largest early-modern azulejo collections in the world.\n\nSeville: Seville is the capital and largest city of the autonomous community of Andalusia and the province of Seville in Spain. It is situated on the lower reaches of the River Guadalquivir, in the southwest of the Iberian Peninsula. Seville has a municipal population of about 688,714 as of 2025, and a metropolitan population of about 1.5 million, making it the largest city in Andalusia and the 5th-largest city in Spain. Its old town, with an area of 4 square kilometres , contains a UNESCO World Heritage Site comprising three buildings: the Alcázar palace complex, the Cathedral and the General Archive of the Indies.", "a": "Thomas Dahlstrøm Nielsen", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_Santa_Catalina,_2022.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/1855_Hollis_St.jpg/1280px-1855_Hollis_St.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2023, "lat": 44.649616, "lng": -63.573906, "p": "Downtown Halifax, Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada", "h": "About this photo: A picture of the front of 1855 Hollis St, Halifax, NS, Canada\n\nNearby: NSCAD University. NSCAD University, also known as the Nova Scotia College of Art and Design , is a public art university in Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada. The university is a co-educational institution that offers bachelor's and master's degrees. The university also provides continuing education services through its School of Extended Studies. The institution was founded by Anna Leonowens in 1887 as the Victoria School of Art and Design. The school was later renamed the Nova Scotia College of Art in 1925. In 1969, the institution was renamed the Nova Scotia College of Art and Design and began to offer undergraduate degrees, becoming the first degree-granting art school in the country.\n\nHalifax: Halifax commonly refers to: Halifax, Nova Scotia, Canada Halifax, West Yorkshire, England Halifax , a British bank Halifax may also refer to:", "a": "Properpostman", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1855_Hollis_St.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Rue_devanr_bureau_des_visas_au_quarier_Abelemkpe_%C3%A0_Accra_au_Ghana_01.jpg/1280px-Rue_devanr_bureau_des_visas_au_quarier_Abelemkpe_%C3%A0_Accra_au_Ghana_01.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2024, "lat": 5.609698, "lng": -0.21192500000001, "p": "Abelenkpe, Accra, Ghana", "h": "About this photo: Rue devanr bureau des visas au quarier Abelemkpe à Accra au Ghana\n\nNearby: Lincoln Community School. Lincoln Community School is a private school in Accra, Ghana, West Africa. It is a non-profit, college-preparatory, international school. There is an expatriate population associated with the various embassies, Foreign Service agencies, NGO's, United Nations organisations, and international businesses. Lincoln Community School is an International Baccalaureate World School offering the Primary Years, Middle Years, and Diploma Programs. The school has a large number of students whose parents are Ghanaian nationals. 25% of the students are Americans, 8% are Ghanaians, and 67% are from other countries. English is the language of instruction. Classes are offered from Pre-school through to grade 12.\n\nAccra: Accra is the capital and largest city of Ghana, located on the southern coast at the Gulf of Guinea, which is part of the Atlantic Ocean. As of the 2021 census, the Accra Metropolitan District, 20.4 km2 , had a population of 284,124, and the larger Greater Accra Region, 3,245 km2 , had a population of 5.46 million. The name \"Accra\" sometimes refers to the territory of the Accra Metropolitan District as it existed before 2008, when it covered 199.4 km2 . This territory has since been split into 13 local government districts: 12 independent municipal districts and the reduced Accra Metropolitan District , which is the only district within the capital to be granted city status.", "a": "Adoscam", "l": "CC0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rue_devanr_bureau_des_visas_au_quarier_Abelemkpe_%C3%A0_Accra_au_Ghana_01.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Building_of_1904_in_Baku.jpg/1280px-Building_of_1904_in_Baku.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 40.382648737389, "lng": 49.871590778278, "p": "Khatay Raion, Azerbaijan", "h": "About this photo: Building in Baku. Built in 1904.\n\nNearby: Church of the Immaculate Conception, Baku. The Church of the Virgin Mary's Immaculate Conception is a Roman Catholic church in Baku, Azerbaijan. The original church, also named Church of the Immaculate Conception operated from 1915 to 1931, when it was ordered destroyed by the Soviet government. In 2006, a new Catholic Church bearing the same name was rebuilt in a different part of Baku. On 29 April 2007, the new church was consecrated by Apostolic Nuncio in Transcaucasia, Monsignor Claudio Gugerotti. The parish is administered by the Salesians of Don Bosco.", "a": "Interfase", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Building_of_1904_in_Baku.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Vancouver_Regal_Hotel_2010.jpg/1280px-Vancouver_Regal_Hotel_2010.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 49.278633333333, "lng": -123.12393888889, "p": "Downtown, Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada", "h": "About this photo: Regal Hotel, 1046 Granville Street, Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada. Architects: Parr and Fee.\n\nNearby: VIFF Centre. The VIFF Centre is a cinema and film centre located at 1181 Seymour Street in downtown Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada, that shows new independent films, international releases, repertory screenings, filmmaker talks, and special cultural events all year. The centre is also home to VIFF , one of Canada’s largest film festivals, which takes place across multiple venues throughout Vancouver each fall. The VIFF Centre houses the state-of-the-art 170-seat VIFF Cinema and the 40-seat Lochmaddy Studio Theatre, as well as the offices for the Vancouver International Film Festival. The facility is available to rent and can accommodate seminars, live performances, film, video, and multimedia presentations.\n\nVancouver: Vancouver is a major city in Western Canada, located in the Lower Mainland region of British Columbia. As the most populous city in the province, the 2021 Canadian census recorded 662,248 people in the city, up from 631,486 in 2016. The Greater Vancouver area had a population of 2.6 million in 2021, making it the third-largest metropolitan area in Canada after Toronto and Montreal. The region, together with the Fraser Valley, makes up the Lower Mainland, which has a total population of over 3 million. Vancouver has the highest population density in Canada, with over 5,700 inhabitants per square kilometre , and the fourth highest in North America .", "a": "Canadian2006", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vancouver_Regal_Hotel_2010.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Tetteh_Quarshie_Interchange_1.jpg/1280px-Tetteh_Quarshie_Interchange_1.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2016, "lat": 5.620682, "lng": -0.17649499999999, "p": "Airport West, Accra, Ghana", "h": "About this photo: Tetteh Quarshie Interchange linking Liberation Road from 37 Military Hospital to the Pantang junction through Madina in Accra, Ghana\n\nNearby: Tetteh Quarshie Interchange. The Tetteh Quarshie Interchange is a cloverleaf interchange carrying a six-lane motorway that links the Liberation Road from 37 Military Hospital to the Pantang junction through Madina in Accra, Ghana. The interchange was commissioned for vehicular use on 27 February 2005 by then president of Ghana, John Agyekum Kufuor.\n\nAccra: Accra is the capital and largest city of Ghana, located on the southern coast at the Gulf of Guinea, which is part of the Atlantic Ocean. As of the 2021 census, the Accra Metropolitan District, 20.4 km2 , had a population of 284,124, and the larger Greater Accra Region, 3,245 km2 , had a population of 5.46 million. The name \"Accra\" sometimes refers to the territory of the Accra Metropolitan District as it existed before 2008, when it covered 199.4 km2 . This territory has since been split into 13 local government districts: 12 independent municipal districts and the reduced Accra Metropolitan District , which is the only district within the capital to be granted city status.", "a": "Masssly", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tetteh_Quarshie_Interchange_1.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/One_Nice_Walk_Along_The_Road_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-One_Nice_Walk_Along_The_Road_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 40.414886, "lng": 49.891125, "p": "Nizami Raion, Baku, Azerbaijan", "h": "About this photo: One Nice Walk Along The Road\n\nNearby: 1995 Baku Metro fire. On 28 October 1995, a fire broke out in the subway system of Baku, the capital of Azerbaijan, between the Ulduz and Nariman Narimanov stations. According to official figures, the fire killed 289 people: 286 passengers and three rescue workers, while 270 people were injured. The fire was deemed to have been caused by an electrical malfunction, but the possibility of sabotage was not excluded. Although a number of people who evacuated the train survived, the fire remains the world's deadliest subway disaster. One person, Chingiz Babayev, was posthumously awarded the title of the National Hero of Azerbaijan for saving passengers' lives.\n\nBaku: Baku is the capital and largest city of Azerbaijan, as well as the largest city on the Caspian Sea and in the Caucasus region. Baku is 28 metres below sea level, which makes it the lowest lying national capital in the world and the largest city in the world below sea level. Baku lies on the southern shore of the Absheron Peninsula, along the Bay of Baku. Baku's urban population is estimated at over two million people. Baku is the primate city of Azerbaijan. It is the sole metropolis in the country, and about 25% of all inhabitants of the country live in Baku's metropolitan area. Baku is divided into twelve administrative raions and 48 townships.", "a": "M. PINARCI", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:One_Nice_Walk_Along_The_Road_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Katembe_bridge.jpg/1280px-Katembe_bridge.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": -25.980537, "lng": 32.559658, "p": "Distrito Municipal de KaTembe, Maputo, Mozambique", "h": "Maputo: Maputo is the capital and largest city of Mozambique. Located near the southern end of the country, it is within 120 kilometres of the borders with Eswatini and South Africa. The city has a population of 1,088,449 distributed over a land area of 347.69 km2 . The Maputo metropolitan area includes the neighbouring city of Matola, and has a total population of 2,717,437. Maputo is a port city, with an economy centered on commerce. It is noted for its vibrant cultural scene and distinctive, eclectic architecture. Maputo was named Lourenço Marques ˈɾẽsu ˈmaɾkɨʃ]) until 1976. Maputo is situated on a large natural bay on the Indian Ocean, near where the rivers Tembe, Mbuluzi, Matola and Infulene converge.", "a": "Robel G. Fucadu", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Katembe_bridge.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4c/Black_Renault_Clio_1997.jpg/1280px-Black_Renault_Clio_1997.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2016, "lat": 38.723119, "lng": -9.139593, "p": "Pena, Lisbon, Portugal", "h": "About this photo: A Renault Clio 1997 in Lisbon,Portugal\n\nNearby: Military Academy (Portugal). The Military Academy is a Portuguese military establishment, which has the ability to confer educational qualifications equivalent to a university. It develops activities of teaching, research and support for the communities with the purpose of training and forming officers for the Portuguese Army and the Republican National Guard.\n\nLisbon: Lisbon is the capital and most populous city of Portugal, with an estimated population of 658,236 as of 2025, within its administrative limits and 3,353,000 within the metropolis, as of 2025. The city lies in the western part of the Iberian Peninsula, fronting the northern shore of the River Tagus. Lisbon is one of the oldest cities in the world and the second-oldest European capital city , predating other modern European capitals by centuries. Settled by pre-Celtic tribes and later founded and civilised by the Phoenicians, Julius Caesar made it a municipium called Felicitas Julia, adding the term to the name Olisipo.", "a": "User3204", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Renault_Clio_1997.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/1992_Mitsubishi_L300_2.4_4WD_Wagon_P24W.jpg/1280px-1992_Mitsubishi_L300_2.4_4WD_Wagon_P24W.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 64.14839, "lng": -21.935618333333, "p": "Miðborg, Reykjavik, Iceland", "h": "About this photo: 1992 Mitsubishi L300 4WD Wagon 2.4 with a 5-speed manual. Chassis number 312, which seems low.\n\nNearby: Icelandic Phallological Museum. The Icelandic Phallological Museum , located in Reykjavík, Iceland, houses the world's largest display of penises and penile parts. It holds over 300 penises from more than 100 species of mammals. The museum also holds 22 imagined penises of creatures from Icelandic folklore. In July 2011, the museum obtained its first human penis, one of many promised by would-be donors. Its detachment from the donor's body did not go according to plan and it was reduced to a greyish-brown shriveled mass that was pickled in a jar of formalin.\n\nReykjavík: Reykjavík is the capital and largest city of Iceland. It is located on the southern shore of the Faxaflói bay in southwest Iceland and has a latitude of 64°08′ N, making it the world's northernmost capital of a sovereign state. Reykjavík has a population of around 140,000 as of 2026, and the surrounding Capital Region has a population of around 252,000, constituting approximately 64% of Iceland's population. According to Landnámabók, the settlement of Iceland began in Reykjavík when Ingólfur Arnarson arrived from Norway in the year 874.", "a": "Mr.choppers", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1992_Mitsubishi_L300_2.4_4WD_Wagon_P24W.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Escola_Secund%C3%A1ria_Francisco_Manyanga_Maputo.JPG/1280px-Escola_Secund%C3%A1ria_Francisco_Manyanga_Maputo.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2015, "lat": -25.9626405568, "lng": 32.5653623342, "p": "Alto Maé \"B\", Maputo, Mozambique", "h": "About this photo: Francisco Manyanga Secondary School, Maputo, Mozambique\n\nMaputo: Maputo is the capital and largest city of Mozambique. Located near the southern end of the country, it is within 120 kilometres of the borders with Eswatini and South Africa. The city has a population of 1,088,449 distributed over a land area of 347.69 km2 . The Maputo metropolitan area includes the neighbouring city of Matola, and has a total population of 2,717,437. Maputo is a port city, with an economy centered on commerce. It is noted for its vibrant cultural scene and distinctive, eclectic architecture. Maputo was named Lourenço Marques ˈɾẽsu ˈmaɾkɨʃ]) until 1976. Maputo is situated on a large natural bay on the Indian Ocean, near where the rivers Tembe, Mbuluzi, Matola and Infulene converge.", "a": "Jcornelius", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Escola_Secund%C3%A1ria_Francisco_Manyanga_Maputo.JPG"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Lhasa_Menzikang_2014.09.23_12-44-08.jpg/1280px-Lhasa_Menzikang_2014.09.23_12-44-08.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2014, "lat": 29.6536916667, "lng": 91.12777, "p": "Barkor Subdistrict, Chengguan District, China", "h": "Nearby: Tsomon Ling. Tsomon Ling, Tsomonling, Tsome Ling, Chomoling is a temple in inner Lhasa, Tibet Autonomous Region, China, south of the Ramoche Temple, and on the corner of one of the main roads, Dekyi Shar Lam. It was one of the Four Royal Colleges or Regency Temples of Lhasa built during the 17th century after the Fifth Dalai Lama assumed both temporal as well as spiritual power. The other three Ling are Tengye Ling, Kunde Ling, and Drib Tsemchok Ling.\n\nChengguan: Chengguan may refer to: Chengguan , an administrative practice of city-level local governments in China Chengguan , fully known as Qingliang Chengguan , Chinese monk, representative of the Huayan school of Chinese Buddhism", "a": "Zhangzhugang", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_Menzikang_2014.09.23_12-44-08.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Ruins_in_Beirut_%284694178315%29.jpg/1280px-Ruins_in_Beirut_%284694178315%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2010, "lat": 33.883669, "lng": 35.506095, "p": "Al Eamiliyeh, Ras El-Nabaa, Lebanon", "h": "Nearby: Beirut Governorate. Beirut Governorate is a Lebanese governorate that consists of one district and one city, Beirut, which is also its capital, and the capital of Lebanon. The area of this governorate is 19.8 km2 ; despite its small size, it is considered the most important region in Lebanon because of its economic, political, cultural, and social activity. The governor of the Beirut Governate is Greek Orthodox according to tradition, while the mayor of the City of Beirut is Sunni Muslim. Beirut is known to be the most religiously diverse city in the Middle East. There are about 2.5 million people in Beirut and its suburbs .", "a": "Francisco Anzola", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_in_Beirut_(4694178315).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Suva%2C_Fiji_17.jpg/1280px-Suva%2C_Fiji_17.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2014, "lat": -18.135622222222, "lng": 178.42979722222, "p": "Toorak, Suva, Fiji", "h": "Nearby: Archdiocese of Suva. The Archdiocese of Suva is a Latin Catholic metropolitan archdiocese in Fiji. It is responsible for the suffragan dioceses of Rarotonga and Tarawa and Nauru and —as of 21 March 2003—the Mission Sui Iuris of Funafuti. The archdiocese was created in 1966 to succeed the Apostolic Vicariate of Fiji.\n\nSuva: Suva is the capital and most populous settlement of Fiji. It is the centre of Fiji's largest metropolitan area and serves as its major port. The city is on the southeast coast of the island of Viti Levu, in Rewa Province, Central Division. In 1877, Fiji's capital was moved to Suva from Levuka, the main European colonial settlement at the time, due to the restrictive geography and environs of the latter. The administration of the colony was transferred from Levuka to Suva in 1882. As of the 2017 census, Suva had a population of 93,874, The urban area which includes Nasinu had a population of 186,920. The metropolitan population of Suva including the towns of Lami, Nasinu, and Nausori was 268,423 .", "a": "Maksym Kozlenko", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suva,_Fiji_17.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Cartagena_%288381219783%29.jpg/1280px-Cartagena_%288381219783%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 10.432442, "lng": -75.518646, "p": "La Paz, Cartagena, Colombia", "h": "About this photo: Barco em Punta Arena. Cartagena de Indias, Colômbia. Rafaela Ely©2012", "a": "Rafaela Ely", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cartagena_(8381219783).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f5/Rail_yard%2C_Baku_%28P1090210%29.jpg/1280px-Rail_yard%2C_Baku_%28P1090210%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": 40.413617333333, "lng": 49.873211, "p": "Nərimanov rayonu, Baku, Azerbaijan", "h": "Baku: Baku is the capital and largest city of Azerbaijan, as well as the largest city on the Caspian Sea and in the Caucasus region. Baku is 28 metres below sea level, which makes it the lowest lying national capital in the world and the largest city in the world below sea level. Baku lies on the southern shore of the Absheron Peninsula, along the Bay of Baku. Baku's urban population is estimated at over two million people. Baku is the primate city of Azerbaijan. It is the sole metropolis in the country, and about 25% of all inhabitants of the country live in Baku's metropolitan area. Baku is divided into twelve administrative raions and 48 townships.", "a": "Matti Blume", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rail_yard,_Baku_(P1090210).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Atardecer_en_el_norte_de_Bogot%C3%A1..jpg/1280px-Atardecer_en_el_norte_de_Bogot%C3%A1..jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 4.725025, "lng": -74.077958333333, "p": "Localidad Suba, Bogota, Colombia", "h": "About this photo: Atardecer en el norte de Bogotá.\n\nNearby: Chibcha terrane. The Chibcha terrane , named after Chibcha, is the largest of the geological provinces of Colombia. The terrane, the oldest explored domains of which date to the Meso- to Neoproterozoic, is situated on the North Andes Plate. The megaregional Romeral Fault System forms the contact of the terrane with the Tahamí terrane. The contact with the Caribbean and La Guajira terranes is formed by the regional Bucaramanga-Santa Marta Fault. The northeastern boundary is formed by the regional Oca Fault, bounding the La Guajira terrane. The terrane is emplaced over the Río Negro–Juruena province of the Amazonian craton along the megaregional Eastern Frontal Fault System.\n\nBogotá: Bogotá is the capital and largest city of Colombia. The city is administered as the Capital District, as well as the capital of, though not politically part of, the surrounding department of Cundinamarca. Bogotá is a territorial entity of the first order, with the same administrative status as the departments of Colombia. It is the main political, economic, administrative, industrial, cultural, aeronautical, technological, scientific, medical, educational and airport center of the country and northern South America.", "a": "Felipeortegag", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atardecer_en_el_norte_de_Bogot%C3%A1..jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Convento_La_Popa_CTG_11_2019_9723.jpg/1280px-Convento_La_Popa_CTG_11_2019_9723.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": 10.416547, "lng": -75.514283, "p": "La Esperanza, Cartagena, Colombia", "h": "About this photo: Vista lejana del Convento de Santa Cruz de la Popa, Cartagena de Indias", "a": "Mariordo (Mario Roberto Durán Ortiz)", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Convento_La_Popa_CTG_11_2019_9723.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Crab_restaurant_%2848885262138%29.jpg/1280px-Crab_restaurant_%2848885262138%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": 35.008785, "lng": 135.766983, "p": "Nakagyo Ward, Kyoto, Japan", "h": "Nearby: Ikedaya incident. The Ikedaya incident , also known as the Ikedaya affair or Ikedaya riot, was an armed encounter between the shishi which included masterless samurai formally employed by the Chōshū, Tosa and Higo domains , and the Shinsengumi, the Bakufu's special police force in Kyoto on July 8, 1864, at the Ikedaya Inn in Sanjō-Kawaramachi, Kyoto, Japan.\n\nKyoto: Kyoto , officially Kyoto City , is the capital city of Kyoto Prefecture in the Kansai region of Japan's largest and most populous island of Honshu. As of 2020, the city had a population of 1.46 million, making it the ninth-most populous city in Japan. More than half of Kyoto Prefecture's population resides in the city. The city is the cultural anchor of the substantially larger Greater Kyoto, a metropolitan statistical area home to approximately 3.8 million people. It is also part of the even larger Keihanshin metropolitan area, along with Osaka and Kobe. Kyoto is one of the oldest municipalities in Japan, having been chosen in 794 as the new seat of Japan's imperial court by Emperor Kanmu.", "a": "Maarten Heerlien from Voorschoten, The Netherlands", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crab_restaurant_(48885262138).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0a/Mapillary_%28cpqg-AAEQ0eh8C1iRHGhUQ%29_%28outbacktesla%29_2018-08-05_02H38M20S253.jpg/1280px-Mapillary_%28cpqg-AAEQ0eh8C1iRHGhUQ%29_%28outbacktesla%29_2018-08-05_02H38M20S253.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": -12.455495023246, "lng": 130.83394571764, "p": "Larrakeyah, Darwin, Australia", "h": "Darwin: Darwin may refer to: Charles Darwin , English naturalist and writer, originator of the theory of biological evolution by natural selection Darwin, Northern Territory, a capital city in Australia, named after the naturalist Darwin may also refer to:", "a": "outbacktesla", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapillary_(cpqg-AAEQ0eh8C1iRHGhUQ)_(outbacktesla)_2018-08-05_02H38M20S253.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Leh_Old_Town_in_2009.jpg/1280px-Leh_Old_Town_in_2009.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": 34.164669, "lng": 77.584183, "p": "Zangsti, Leh, Ladakh, India", "h": "Nearby: Ladakh. Ladakh is a region administered by India as a union territory and constitutes an eastern portion of the larger Kashmir region that has been the subject of a dispute between India and Pakistan since 1947 and India and China since 1959. Ladakh is bordered by the Tibet Autonomous Region of China to the east, the Indian state of Himachal Pradesh to the south, both the Indian-administered union territory of Jammu and Kashmir and the Pakistani-administered Gilgit-Baltistan to the west, and the southwest corner of Xinjiang across the Karakoram Pass in the far north. It extends from the Siachen Glacier in the Karakoram range to the north to the main Great Himalayas to the south.\n\nLeh: Leh is a city in Indian-administered Ladakh in the disputed Kashmir region. It is the largest city and the joint capital of Ladakh. Leh, located in the Leh district, has been the capital of Ladakh since the medieval period and was historically capital of the Kingdom of Ladakh. The seat of the kingdom, Leh Palace, the former residence of the royal family of Ladakh, was built in the 17th-century and is frequently contrasted with the Potala Palace in Tibet. Leh is at an altitude of 3,524 m , and is connected via National Highway 1 to Srinagar in the southwest and to Manali in the south via the Leh-Manali Highway .", "a": "Kiran Jonnalagadda from Bangalore, India", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leh_Old_Town_in_2009.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/View_from_Federated_Place%2C_Hillbrow%2C_Johannesburg_%281980%29_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-View_from_Federated_Place%2C_Hillbrow%2C_Johannesburg_%281980%29_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2009, "lat": -26.190346, "lng": 28.052162, "p": "Hillbrow, Johannesburg, South Africa", "h": "About this photo: View from Federated Place, Hillbrow, Johannesburg\n\nNearby: Hillbrow. Hillbrow is an Inner City residential neighbourhood of Johannesburg, Gauteng, South Africa. It is characterized by a high population density and has experienced issues associated with municipal disinvestment, including elevated levels of unemployment, poverty, prostitution, and crime. Under the Group Areas Act during apartheid, Hillbrow was initially designated a \"whites-only\" area. It later became a \"grey area\", where residents of different ethnic backgrounds lived together. During this period, it developed a reputation as a cosmopolitan neighbourhood with a politically progressive character and was one of the first recognized queer neighborhoods in South Africa.\n\nJohannesburg: Johannesburg , colloquially known as Jozi, Joburg, or \"the City of Gold\", is the capital city of the province of Gauteng and the largest city in South Africa. In 2022 , the City of Johannesburg Metropolitan Municipality had a population of 4,803,262, and had a wider metropolitan area of 15,099,422, making it one of the 100 largest urban areas in the world. Despite not being the country’s official judicial capital , it is the seat of the country's highest court, the Constitutional Court. Situated on the mineral-rich Witwatersrand, the city has long been at the epicentre of the international mineral and gold trade.", "a": "qwesy qwesy", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Federated_Place,_Hillbrow,_Johannesburg_(1980)_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/56/QUO_-_square.jpg/1280px-QUO_-_square.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2021, "lat": 14.618301, "lng": -90.51509, "p": "El Edén, Guatemala City, Guatemala", "h": "Nearby: Central American Parliament. The Central American Parliament , also known as PARLACEN, is the political institution and parliamentary body of the Central American Integration System . Its headquarters are in Guatemala City.\n\nGuatemala City: Guatemala City , also known colloquially by the nickname Guate, is the national capital and largest city of the Republic of Guatemala. It serves as the municipal capital of the surrounding Guatemala Department. Its metropolitan area is also the largest in Central America. The city is located in a mountain valley called Valle de la Ermita in the south-central part of the country. Guatemala City is the site of the native Mayan city of Kaminaljuyu in Mesoamerica, which was occupied primarily between 1500 BCE and 1200 CE. The present city was founded by the Spanish after their colonial capital, now called Antigua Guatemala was destroyed by the devastating 1773 Santa Marta earthquake and its aftershocks.", "a": "Rene Hernandez", "l": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:QUO_-_square.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/51/Section_5_Xinyi_Road_20200329.jpg/1280px-Section_5_Xinyi_Road_20200329.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2020, "lat": 25.032553, "lng": 121.573688, "p": "Xinyi District, Taipei, Taiwan", "h": "About this photo: Section 5 Xinyi Road 20200329\n\nNearby: Xiangshan metro station. The Taipei Metro Xiangshan station is a metro station on the Red Line located in Xinyi, Taipei, Taiwan. The station opened on November 24, 2013.\n\nTaipei: Taipei , officially Taipei City, is the capital and a special municipality of Taiwan. Located in Northern Taiwan, Taipei City is an enclave of the municipality of New Taipei City that sits about 25 kilometres southwest of the northern port city of Keelung. Most of the city rests on the Taipei Basin, an ancient lakebed. The basin is bounded by the relatively narrow valleys of the Keelung and Xindian rivers, which join to form the Tamsui River along the city's western border.", "a": "MiNe", "l": "CC BY 2.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Section_5_Xinyi_Road_20200329.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Barrio_el_socorro_familia_jimenes_-_panoramio.jpg/1280px-Barrio_el_socorro_familia_jimenes_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": 10.38105, "lng": -75.483181, "p": "El Socorro, Cartagena, Colombia", "h": "About this photo: barrio el socorro familia jimenes", "a": "kosmel bonfante", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barrio_el_socorro_familia_jimenes_-_panoramio.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/View_of_Ushuaia.jpg/1280px-View_of_Ushuaia.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2014, "lat": -54.815086111111, "lng": -68.3062, "p": "Costa Centro, Ushuaia, Argentina", "h": "About this photo: View of Ushuaia, Tierra del Fuego, Argentina\n\nUshuaia: Ushuaia is the capital of Tierra del Fuego, Antártida e Islas del Atlántico Sur Province, Argentina. With a population of 89,606 and a location below the 54th parallel south latitude, Ushuaia claims the title of world's southernmost city. Ushuaia is located in a wide bay on the southern coast of Isla Grande de Tierra del Fuego. It is bounded on the north by the Martial mountain range and on the south by the Beagle Channel. It is the only municipality in the Department of Ushuaia and has an area of 9,390 km2 . It was founded on 12 October 1884 by Augusto Lasserre and is located on the shores of the Beagle Channel surrounded by the mountain range of the Martial Glacier, in the Bay of Ushuaia.", "a": "Justraveling.com", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Ushuaia.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/1983_-_panoramio_-_z-pinch.jpg/1280px-1983_-_panoramio_-_z-pinch.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2011, "lat": 9.011959, "lng": 38.738506, "p": "Lideta, Ethiopia", "h": "Nearby: Lideta. Lideta is a district of Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. As of 2011 its population was of 214,769.", "a": "z-pinch", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1983_-_panoramio_-_z-pinch.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Cartagena%2C_Colombia_-_Laslovarga_%2858%29.jpg/1280px-Cartagena%2C_Colombia_-_Laslovarga_%2858%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2017, "lat": 10.423217, "lng": -75.539671, "p": "El Espinal, Cartagena, Colombia", "h": "About this photo: Cartagena, Colombia. Castillo de San Felipe de Barajas\n\nNearby: Castillo San Felipe de Barajas. The Castillo San Felipe de Barajas is a fortress in the city of Cartagena, Colombia. Constructed in 1639, it is located on Cerro San Lázaro in a location that overlooks approaches to the city by land or sea. It was originally known as the Castillo de San Lázaro. It was built by African slave labor under Spanish supervision during the colonial era. The fortress was involved in several battles between the late 17th to early 19th centuries between European powers.", "a": "Laslovarga", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cartagena,_Colombia_-_Laslovarga_(58).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Ph%E1%BB%95_Quang_Pagoda%2C_Ho_Chi_Minh_City.jpg/1280px-Ph%E1%BB%95_Quang_Pagoda%2C_Ho_Chi_Minh_City.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2018, "lat": 10.80485, "lng": 106.668419, "p": "Phường Tân Sơn Hòa, Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam", "h": "About this photo: Phổ Quang Pagoda in Ho Chi Minh City.\n\nNearby: Quân khu 7 Stadium. Quân khu 7 Stadium is a stadium in Tân Bình District and part of the Headquarters of 7th Military Region or simply called as the area of 7th Military Region, a military area mostly in the adjacented Phú Nhuận District, it's 10 minutes away from Tan Son Nhat International Airport, Ho Chi Minh City. It has a capacity of around 25,000 people.\n\nHo Chi Minh City: Ho Chi Minh City , formerly Saigon , is the most populous city and centrally-governed city of Vietnam, with a population of more than 14 million in 2025. Its geography is defined by rivers and canals, of which the largest is the Saigon River. As the biggest financial centre of Vietnam, Ho Chi Minh City has the largest gross regional domestic product out of all Vietnamese provinces and municipalities, contributing around a quarter of the country's total GDP. It is the busiest international transport hub in Vietnam, with Tân Sơn Nhất International Airport accounting for nearly half of all international arrivals to Vietnam, and the Port of Saigon among the busiest container ports in Southeast Asia.", "a": "Christophe95", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ph%E1%BB%95_Quang_Pagoda,_Ho_Chi_Minh_City.jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/La_Habana_Vieja_%28165686455%29.jpeg/1280px-La_Habana_Vieja_%28165686455%29.jpeg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2016, "lat": 23.1135925, "lng": -82.3665956, "p": "Santos Suárez, Havana, Cuba", "h": "About this photo: 500px provided description: La Habana Vieja [#cuba ,#habana ,#havana ,#la habana ,#cuban ,#street photography]\n\nHavana: Havana is the capital and largest city of Cuba. The heart of La Habana Province, Havana is the country's main port and commercial center. It is the most populous city, the largest by area, and the second-largest metropolitan area in the Caribbean region. The population in 2021 was 2,142,939 inhabitants, and its area is 728.26 km2 for the capital city and 8,475.57 km2 for the metropolitan zone. Its official population dropped to 1,749,964 inhabitants by the end of 2024. Founded in 1519 by the Spanish Empire, Havana overtook Santiago in the mid-16th century due to the geostrategic advantages of its harbor, becoming the capital of the island in 1552.", "a": "Dani E Pérez", "l": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Habana_Vieja_(165686455).jpeg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fb/Red_Building_-_panoramio_%281%29.jpg/1280px-Red_Building_-_panoramio_%281%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2013, "lat": 6.935304, "lng": 79.844974, "p": "Fort, Colombo, Sri Lanka", "h": "Nearby: Former General Post Office, Colombo. The former Colombo General Post Office , at 17 Janadhipathi Mawatha, Colombo Fort, was the headquarters of the Sri Lanka Post and the office of the Postmaster General for over one hundred years, from 1895 until 2000.\n\nColombo: Colombo is Sri Lanka's executive and judicial capital, as well as the country's most populous city. It is also the financial centre of the island and a tourist destination. The Colombo metropolitan area is estimated to have a population of 5.6 million, and 752,993 within the municipal limits. It is located on the west coast of the island. It is also the administrative capital of the Western Province and the district capital of Colombo District. It was made the capital of the island when Sri Lanka was ceded to the British Empire in 1815, retaining its capital status when Sri Lanka gained independence in 1948.", "a": "Pol van den Scheetek…", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Building_-_panoramio_(1).jpg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Crowded_Ho_Chi_Minh_%28135053309%29.jpeg/1280px-Crowded_Ho_Chi_Minh_%28135053309%29.jpeg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2012, "lat": 10.8230989, "lng": 106.6296638, "p": "Phường Tân Sơn, Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam", "h": "About this photo: 500px provided description: Crowded street in Ho Chi Minh City, Vietnam [#city ,#asia ,#vietnam ,#ho chi minh city ,#ho chi]\n\nHo Chi Minh City: Ho Chi Minh City , formerly Saigon , is the most populous city and centrally-governed city of Vietnam, with a population of more than 14 million in 2025. Its geography is defined by rivers and canals, of which the largest is the Saigon River. As the biggest financial centre of Vietnam, Ho Chi Minh City has the largest gross regional domestic product out of all Vietnamese provinces and municipalities, contributing around a quarter of the country's total GDP. It is the busiest international transport hub in Vietnam, with Tân Sơn Nhất International Airport accounting for nearly half of all international arrivals to Vietnam, and the Port of Saigon among the busiest container ports in Southeast Asia.", "a": "Matheus Riolfi", "l": "CC BY 3.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowded_Ho_Chi_Minh_(135053309).jpeg"}, {"u": "https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Centro_Hist%C3%B3rico_Ciudad_de_Guatemala.jpg/1280px-Centro_Hist%C3%B3rico_Ciudad_de_Guatemala.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail", "y": 2019, "lat": 14.635964, "lng": -90.514747, "p": "Centro Histórico, Guatemala City, Guatemala", "h": "About this photo: Centro Histórico Ciudad de Guatemala\n\nNearby: Guatemala Post Office Building. The Guatemala Post Office Building is a building in Guatemala City, Guatemala. It is the management centre of postal services in Guatemala. It was constructed between 1937 and 1940. The characteristic archway of the building was inspired by the Santa Catalina Arch in Antigua Guatemala.\n\nGuatemala City: Guatemala City , also known colloquially by the nickname Guate, is the national capital and largest city of the Republic of Guatemala. It serves as the municipal capital of the surrounding Guatemala Department. Its metropolitan area is also the largest in Central America. The city is located in a mountain valley called Valle de la Ermita in the south-central part of the country. Guatemala City is the site of the native Mayan city of Kaminaljuyu in Mesoamerica, which was occupied primarily between 1500 BCE and 1200 CE. The present city was founded by the Spanish after their colonial capital, now called Antigua Guatemala was destroyed by the devastating 1773 Santa Marta earthquake and its aftershocks.", "a": "Marcelo Legrand", "l": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "page": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Centro_Hist%C3%B3rico_Ciudad_de_Guatemala.jpg"}];
